course: dictionary
lesson: 6603
title: Nominally: in name only
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you that what something is called is not the same as what it really is. Nominally is one of them. It means in name only, on paper, but not really or not in practice. «He is nominally in charge, but his deputy does the work.» He has the title of boss, but the deputy is the one doing the real job.
explain: Look for clue words. The sentence has but, which turns the first half around: nominally in charge, but the deputy does the work. When you see a word like this, ask: is the sentence saying something is true on paper, and then saying it is not true in real life?
explain: In money, nominally means the amount written on something, before anything else is counted. «The coin is nominally worth £1» means the number printed on it is £1, even if what it can really buy is different. To check your meaning, swap in "in name only" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Nominally means in name only, or on paper, but not really in practice.
fact: Nominally is an adverb. It tells you how something is, in name, and it often comes before words like in charge, under or independent.
fact: A nominal value is the number printed on something, not always what it can actually buy.
fact: «Troops nominally (on paper) under his command» means the soldiers were officially listed under him, which is not the same as him really controlling them.
fact: Clue words such as but often show that a word like nominally is coming: the first part says one thing, and the second part tells you the real truth.
exercise: «He is nominally in charge, but his deputy does the work.» What does nominally mean here? | Very carefully and with great skill | Loudly, so everyone can hear | Happily and without any fuss | *In name only, not really in practice
exercise: «The coin is nominally worth £1.» In money, what does nominally mean? | *The amount printed on it, before other things are counted | The amount it can buy in every shop | The amount it would cost to make the coin
exercise: «The club is nominally run by the head teacher, but the students make all the choices.» Which word is the clue that nominally is about to change the meaning? | choices | club | students | *but
test: «The king is nominally the ruler, but the council makes all the laws.» What does nominally suggest? | Happily and gladly | *Only in name, not really in practice | Wearing a crown every day | Very strongly and firmly
test: Which sentence shows someone is in charge only on paper? | Dad cooked every meal himself, and he loves it | Dad is very tall for his age | *Dad is nominally the chef, but Mum does all the cooking | Dad went to the shop to buy some bread
exam: «The prince was nominally the leader of the tribe, but his mother made every decision.» What does nominally mean in this sentence? | Fully and completely in charge | Sadly and quietly | Quickly, in a hurry | *In name only, but not really leading
